\cf0 A callable\cell 
\pard\intbl\itap1\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf0 Custom function that returns the error message\cell \lastrow\row
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0
\cf0 With 
\f3\fs26 handle_errors=False
\f1\fs24  the notebook simply raised 
\f3\fs26 ValidationError
\f1\fs24  and stopped. With 
\f3\fs26 True
\f1\fs24  it recovered. As a developer you're paid to keep the code from crashing.\
A caution from the demo: a vague custom string ("please make sure you follow the exact structure") sent the model round in circles and burned tokens. A specific one ("ticket count should not be greater than 10") resolved in two calls. The default Pydantic error is usually better than anything you'd write by hand.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa280\partightenfactor0

\f0\b\fs28 \cf0 Why not just clamp the value?\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f1\b0\fs24 \cf0 The obvious "if count > 10, set it to 10" fails for the same reason validation exists: the user might have asked for 8 and the model returned 80. You want the model corrected, not the output silently rewritten.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa280\partightenfactor0

\f0\b\fs28 \cf0 Retry count\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f1\b0\fs24 \cf0 There's no retry-limit parameter exposed. In practice it resolves in two or three passes. If you need a hard cap, write a custom error-handler function and count inside it.\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf3 \strokec3 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa298\partightenfactor0

\f0\b\fs36 \cf0 \strokec2 8. Why a bare model isn't enough\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f1\b0\fs24 \cf0 Bind tools 
\f0\b and
\f1\b0  a structured output schema to a raw model, then invoke it:\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\f3\fs26 \cf0 incomplete_model = model.bind_tools([check_showtimes]).with_structured_output(BookingRequest)\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f1\fs24 \cf0 The model returned the booking request and never touched the tool. One request, one round trip, one response. The model can tell you which tool to call 
\f5\i or
\f1\i0  produce structured output, but it cannot loop.\

